  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nWidespread high cloud coverage is being observed at 12 PM on \nvisible satellite. Light radar returns are being observed in the \ndeserts and mountains, although only an isolated hundredth of rain\nhas been observed in area rain gauges. Any additional rain that \nfalls should just be a few sprinkles and will not have any \nimpacts. Showers chances continue to look unlikely through \nWednesday.\n\nThe weather pattern this week will be mainly influenced by broad \nupper level ridging across the southwest United States. Today and \nTuesday will see minor day to day changes in temperatures with highs \nremaining near to slightly above normal as the high drifts east over \nthe Midwest. Starting Wednesday, the upper level high is forecast to \nshift south and west more over New Mexico and Texas which is when it \nwill begin to have more of an influence on our weather. Highs on \nWednesday will be 5 to 10 degrees warmer than Tuesday for inland \nlocations and a few degrees warmer along the  coast. Conditions will \ncontinue to warm Thursday through the upcoming weekend as the upper \nlevel high's position moves more west over Arizona/Nevada. \n\nFor the middle of the week through the upcoming weekend, both high \nand low temperatures will be running 5 to 10 degrees above average. \nThe combination of elevated high and low temperatures will limit the \nability to recover from heat overnight and allow for conditions to \nquickly warm once the sun rises. Widespread moderate to locally \nmoderate HeatRisk is expected in inland Orange County, San Diego \nValleys, Inland Empire, and area mountains. Major HeatRisk is \nexpected in the deserts. Major HeatRisk indicates that the \ngeneral population may be impacted by the heat if precautions are \nnot taken. \n\nWhile there remains chances of showers and thunderstorms in the \nmountains Thursday and Friday afternoons, chances are continuing to \ndecrease over the last few forecast cycles. Guidance is also \nindicating a decrease in precipitable water values, with the GFS \nindicating a more notable decrease than the ECMWF ensemble guidance. \nLess of a moisture influx would allow for a greater warming, while \nmore moisture would put a limit on how warm highs will get it would \nalso increase humidity which would make conditions muggier.",
